  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nIt was a beautiful Sunday for many with sunshine returning by\nthe late afternoon and evening. Unfortunately this will be\nshort-lived as low clouds and patchy fog redevelop tonight. Low\nclouds and fog are expected to linger into the late morning and\nearly afternoon, but a few breaks in the clouds may again be\npossible by the late afternoon and evening on Monday. Outside \nof a damp morning, the weather will remain dry with highs in the\n70s.\n\nThe dry weather comes to a swift end Tuesday morning. A large\nupper-level trough near the Four Corners begins to ingest rich\ntropical moisture from the remnants of Polo. As this occurs, an\narea of surface low pressure develops ahead of these features \nover the south-central Plains before lifting northeast towards \nour region on Wednesday. The upper-level trough does not move \nover the area until Thursday afternoon. A cold front will then \npush through the region, sending rain south, and bringing and \nend to the rainfall. Widespread showers and thunderstorms (90%+ \nchance) are anticipated through this time frame. With \nsignificant rainfall totals likely for the region. Temperatures \nwill remain in the 60s to low 70s each day due to continued \nrain and cloud cover.\n\nDiving into the details of this rainfall, some uncertainty\nremains, but confidence is increasing in widespread rainfall \ntotals of 2-4\"+ for the area. These rainfall totals will be \nfueled by a warm, moist airmass more typical of the gulf coast \nrather than Fall in the Plains. Despite limited instability, \nwarm rain processes will favor high rainfall rates in any \nshowers and thunderstorms that develop. That said, the exact \ntrack of the area of low pressure and the coinciding region of \ngreatest rainfall continues to shift slightly with the latest \nforecasts. In either case, a flash flood threat may develop if a\ncorridor of repeated heavy rainfall develops. One mitigating \nfactor to this scenario is the duration and placement of \nheaviest rainfall may be short-lived minimizing rainfall totals\nfor any particular area. With that being said, the ceiling for \nthis event remains high and rainfall amounts could come in \nhigher than anticipated in a higher-end scenario. This will \nbe closely monitored as confidence increases with convective \nmodels coming into range for this event. While the flash flood \nthreat remains more conditional, river flooding is a more \nlikely hazard. Area rivers and streams have already seen rises \ndue to recent rainfall and may be primed for flooding with this \nnext event. If you are located in a flood plain, monitor your \nriver forecast closely as flooding may become a concern next\nweek.\n\nOnce this system clears the area on Thursday, a cooler and drier\nFall airmass fills in across the Plains. Even though we expect \nsunshine to return to the area behind this system, cool \ntemperatures linger into next weekend. We can expect highs in \nthe 60s with lows in the low 50s to upper 40s. These \ntemperatures would be some of the coolest since late Spring. \nWhile significant, these temperatures are too warm for any frost\npotential and a delayed first frost compared to average \ncontinues for the area.",
